1 Overview
**********

AI coding agents such as Claude Code
(https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code) and Codex
(https://openai.com/index/introducing-codex/) store complete
conversation transcripts as JSONL files.  While these files contain a
full record of every session, they are not human-readable: each line is
a JSON object with nested content arrays, tool-use blocks, thinking
sections, and system metadata.  ‘agent-log.el’ renders these transcripts
into readable Markdown files and provides a dedicated Emacs interface
for browsing, searching, and navigating them.

   What counts as a session is not Agent Log's decision.  Each backend
reports exactly the conversations its own tool offers when you run its
resume command, and Agent Log lists those and nothing else.  Codex is
asked directly, over app-server ‘thread/list’; Claude Code's own session
record is read from ‘history.jsonl’ and its project transcript
directories.  Agent Log never decides for itself which transcript files
on disk amount to a conversation.  This matters because both tools write
transcript files that are not conversations: Codex writes a separate
rollout file for every subagent, permission guardian, and
non-interactive execution thread, each inheriting the parent's working
directory, and Claude Code writes subagent transcripts under a
‘subagents/’ subdirectory.  Because neither tool lists those in its own
resume picker, they never reach Agent Log, and no heuristic is needed to
tell them apart from real conversations.

   The package supports multiple backends through a generic interface
based on ‘cl-defstruct’ and ‘cl-defmethod’.  Each backend knows how to
discover sessions, parse its tool's JSONL format, and normalize entries
into a canonical representation that the rendering pipeline, session
browser, and AI features operate on.  Currently supported backends are
Claude Code (‘agent-log-claude.el’) and Codex (‘agent-log-codex.el’).
By default, the package auto-detects which tools are installed and
activates the corresponding backends (*note Backend configuration::).

   The package maintains a mirror directory of pre-rendered Markdown
files, organized by project.  This means that standard tools such as
‘consult-ripgrep’, Dired, and ‘grep’ work natively on readable content
without any special integration.

   The core capabilities of ‘agent-log.el’ are:

   • *Session browsing*.  A ‘completing-read’ interface lists ordinary
     user sessions, grouped by project or as a flat list, sorted by
     recency.  Each candidate shows a backend-specific icon (SVG in GUI
     frames, a short text label in terminal frames), the date, project
     name, JSONL file size, and either an AI-generated summary or the
     first user message (*note Session browsing::).
   • *Lazy rendering*.  Sessions are rendered to ‘.md’ files on first
     access.  The package tracks JSONL file sizes in an index to avoid
     re-rendering files that have not changed (*note Sync and
     rendering::).
   • *Live updates*.  For active sessions, a ‘file-notify’ watcher
     detects JSONL changes and appends new entries to both the ‘.md’
     file on disk and the open buffer in real time (*note Live
     updates::).
   • *Outline navigation*.  Rendered buffers use ‘outline-minor-mode’
     for folding tool calls, thinking blocks, and tool results.
     Dedicated keybindings jump between conversation turns (*note
     Navigation::).
   • *Smart tool summaries*.  Each tool call is summarized by its most
     relevant input—the file path for Read/Write, the command for Bash,
     the pattern for Grep, and so on.
   • *AI-powered session summaries*.  An optional integration with
     ‘gptel’ generates concise one-line and paragraph summaries for each
     session, displayed in the browser and at the top of rendered
     buffers (*note Summary generation::).
   • *AI-powered search*.  A two-stage LLM pipeline lets you search your
     session history with natural language queries.  A scope-narrowing
     model filters sessions by project and date range, then a selection
     model identifies the most relevant sessions and presents a
     narrative with clickable links (*note AI search::).
   • *Session resumption*.  You can resume any session directly in the
     coding agent from within the rendered buffer, provided the
     corresponding Emacs integration package is installed (e.g.
     ‘claude-code’ for Claude Code) (*note Session resumption::).

   The package requires Emacs 29.1 or later, markdown-mode
(https://jblevins.org/projects/markdown-mode/) 2.6 or later, and
transient (https://github.com/magit/transient) 0.13.3 or later.  The
optional summary feature requires gptel
(https://github.com/karthink/gptel).  Session resumption for Claude Code
sessions requires claude-code
(https://github.com/stevemolitor/claude-code.el); for Codex sessions it
requires codex (https://github.com/benthamite/codex.el).

   The development repository is on GitHub
(https://github.com/benthamite/agent-log).


