4.1 Session browsing
====================

The project and session pickers remain usable if the buffer from which
you opened them is closed while you are choosing an entry.

   The command ‘agent-log-browse-sessions’ is the primary entry point
for exploring your session history.  It asks each active backend for its
canonical session catalog, resolves each session to its exact JSONL
file, and presents the results in a ‘completing-read’ prompt.  For
Codex, the catalog comes from app-server ‘thread/list’.  Agent Log
follows native Resume's database-first path so browsing never waits for
a scan of the rollout archive.  If the index is stale, the current
indexed catalog is shown immediately while app-server runs its normal
scan-and-repair path in the background; another browse picks up the
repaired index.  Repairs for the same newest rollout are rate-limited,
so an active or otherwise temporarily unindexed session cannot restart
an archive scan on every invocation.  The index is stale when it is
empty, and also when it omits the newest rollout on disk: Codex indexes
a thread when its session closes, so a session whose process was killed
rather than closed — as ‘agent-restart’ does — would otherwise stay out
of the browser for good.  Checking this costs a few directory reads,
because rollouts sit in a date-partitioned tree under names that begin
with the session timestamp.  Subagent rollouts are skipped when looking
for the newest one: Codex never indexes them, so measuring the index
against one would report it stale forever.  In both cases app-server
remains the membership authority: Agent Log applies no rule of its own
and never infers sessions from rollout files.  Each candidate is
formatted as:

     [icon] 2026-02-22 14:06  my-project  42k  "Fix the authentication bug in..."

   Each candidate is prefixed with a backend-specific icon so you can
tell at a glance which agent a session belongs to.  In GUI frames the
icon is an inline SVG image; in terminal frames it falls back to a short
text label (e.g.  ‘CC’ for Claude Code, ‘CX’ for Codex).  Each distinct
backend icon is rendered once per candidate set, using frame metrics
without selecting a window, so candidate construction does not run
unrelated window-selection hooks once per session.  The size column
shows the human-readable size of the source JSONL file.

   When ‘agent-log-group-by-project’ is non-‘nil’ (the default), the
command first prompts for a project, then for a session within that
project (*note Session browser options::).  Sessions are sorted
most-recent-first using ‘agent-log-session-sort-key’.  If AI summaries
have been generated for any sessions (*note Summary generation::), the
summary replaces the quoted first message in the candidate display.  If
the displayed session set still contains sessions without usable
summaries while automatic summarization is enabled, browsing starts a
non-overlapping background sweep that prioritizes that visible set, so
the raw first-message entries are backfilled without waiting for AI
search.

   Every archive-wide command reads that same catalog, so what the
session browser, ‘agent-log-open-latest’, bulk synchronization, archive
summarization, background summary sweeps, and AI search treat as a
session is exactly what the coding agent itself treats as a resumable
conversation.  This matters most for Codex, which writes a separate
rollout file for every subagent, permission guardian, and
non-interactive execution thread, all inheriting the parent's working
directory.  Codex leaves those out of its own thread list, so they never
reach Agent Log and no heuristic is needed to recognize them.

   Within a single browse run, source file size and modification-time
metadata is cached per session.  This keeps flat browsing and
modification-time sorting responsive even when the archive contains many
sessions.  Checks for live sessions reuse the catalog that is already in
hand instead of asking the backend for its session list a second time.
Live-state annotations use one snapshot of the agent package's session
registry for the entire picker, rather than rescanning live buffers for
each historical row.

   Each project is labelled by the shortest trailing portion of its
working directory that uniquely identifies it.  A project whose final
directory component is unique is shown by that component alone (for
example, ‘my-project’); when several projects share a final component,
each label grows one parent directory at a time until it is distinct
(for example, ‘8.1.0-dev/elpaca/sources/codex’ versus
‘8.3.0-dev/elpaca/sources/codex’).  This keeps labels short while
guaranteeing that two distinct project directories never collapse to the
same entry.  Labels that are still wider than
‘agent-log-project-column-max-width’ are abbreviated in the browser
(*note Session browser options::).

   Selecting a session lazily renders it to a ‘.md’ file if needed, then
opens the rendered buffer in ‘agent-log-mode’ (*note agent-log-mode::).

   The command ‘agent-log-open-latest’ opens the most recent session
directly, without prompting.  This is convenient when you want to review
the last conversation you had with a coding agent.

   The command ‘agent-log-open-file’ opens a specific JSONL file by
path.  It prompts for a file path interactively.  This is useful for
automation, for opening session files you found via the filesystem, or
when you know the exact file you want.

   The command ‘agent-log-open-session’ opens a session by its ID.  It
prompts for the session ID interactively.

   The command ‘agent-log-open-current-session’ opens the log for the
coding agent session running in the current buffer.  When the *note
agent integration: Agent integration. is active, the buffer's
authoritative native session id names the exact JSONL file first, which
uniquely identifies the session even when multiple sessions (including
branches created with ‘/branch’) share the same project directory.
Otherwise the command dispatches on the current buffer's backend: if the
buffer is a Claude Code terminal, it matches distinctive visible
terminal text against the project's JSONL transcripts, then falls back
to the most recently modified JSONL file in the project's session
directory, or to searching ‘history.jsonl’.  If the buffer is a Codex
terminal, ‘agent-log’ records the session ID reported by Codex hook
events and uses that buffer-local ID first.  Resolving the buffer's
session reads the Codex thread catalog only when the buffer's own
records cannot name it, so the common case does not start an app-server
process.  Buffers launched with an explicit ‘codex resume SESSION_ID’
command open that session ID directly, even if the resumed session was
originally spawned as a subagent.  Otherwise the backend matches a Codex
session whose recorded working directory matches the buffer's directory
and whose ‘session_meta’ timestamp is near the live terminal process
start time.  If that launch-time match fails but the terminal still
displays transcript text from a completed Codex session, the backend
normalizes distinctive visible lines (for example, terminal continuation
indentation, list markers, and Markdown backticks), matches them back to
the same project's JSONL files, and opens the transcript that actually
appears in the buffer.  This lets you quickly jump from an active or
recently completed coding agent session to its rendered log without
attaching a buffer to an unrelated child or stale session.


