4.2 Rendered directory
======================

The command ‘agent-log-open-directory’ opens the rendered Markdown
directory in Dired.  Since rendered files are plain Markdown organized
by project, you can use standard Emacs tools to browse, search, and
manage them.  For example, you can run ‘consult-ripgrep’ from this Dired
buffer to search across all rendered sessions.

   If the rendered directory does not yet exist, the command creates it.


File: agent-log.info,  Node: Sync and rendering,  Next: Navigation,  Prev: Rendered directory,  Up: Commands

4.3 Sync and rendering
======================

By default, rendering is lazy: sessions are rendered to ‘.md’ files on
first access via ‘agent-log-browse-sessions’ or ‘agent-log-open-file’.
The package maintains an index file (‘_index.el’) in the rendered
directory that maps session IDs to rendered file paths and JSONL file
sizes.  Rendered ownership is checked using the backend, full session
ID, and exact source path.  When you access a session, the package
compares the current JSONL file size and canonical rendered path against
the indexed values; if either differs, the session is re-rendered.

   Rendered output never contains NUL characters: any NUL that appears
in conversation content (for example, in captured terminal output from a
build tool) is replaced with the visible ‘␀’ symbol.  A single NUL byte
would otherwise make Emacs visit the rendered file as binary, breaking
display of non-ASCII characters and crashing integrations that serialize
the buffer content as JSON.

   The command ‘agent-log-sync-sessions’ renders all ordinary,
non-ignored sessions that are unrendered or stale.  It processes
sessions one at a time using ‘run-with-timer’ to avoid blocking Emacs,
then commits the shared index once.  When a session's rendered path
changes, the superseded file it owned is moved to the operating system
Trash; no other rendered file is touched.  Open session buffers follow
the new rendered path and source watcher before the old file is removed,
so live updates and buffer cleanup continue using the canonical file.
The completion callback reports a structured success or failure result,
including the stage that failed.

   The command ‘agent-log-refresh’ re-renders the session displayed in
the current buffer from its JSONL source.  This is useful if the
rendered file appears stale or if you have changed display settings such
as ‘agent-log-show-thinking’ or ‘agent-log-show-tools’ (*note Display of
thinking and tool sections::).  The keybinding is ‘g’ in
‘agent-log-mode’.


