4.4 Navigation
==============

The command ‘agent-log-next-turn’ moves point to the next User or
Assistant heading in the buffer.  The command ‘agent-log-previous-turn’
moves to the previous one.  These commands search for ‘## User’ and ‘##
Assistant’ headings, skipping over tool calls and thinking sections.

   The keybindings are ‘n’ and ‘p’ in ‘agent-log-mode’, making it easy
to step through a conversation turn by turn.


File: agent-log.info,  Node: Section visibility,  Next: Copying,  Prev: Navigation,  Up: Commands

4.5 Section visibility
======================

The command ‘agent-log-toggle-section’ toggles the visibility of the
section at point.  When point is on a ‘####’ heading (tool call, tool
result, or thinking block), it collapses or expands just that section.
When point is on any other heading, it delegates to
‘outline-toggle-children’.  The keybinding is ‘TAB’.

   The command ‘agent-log-collapse-all’ collapses all tool-use,
tool-result, and thinking sections in the buffer at once.  This gives
you a clean view of just the user messages and assistant text responses.
The keybinding is ‘C’.

   The command ‘agent-log-expand-all’ expands all sections, removing all
section overlays and showing the full content.  The keybinding is ‘E’.


File: agent-log.info,  Node: Copying,  Next: Summary generation,  Prev: Section visibility,  Up: Commands

4.6 Copying
===========

The command ‘agent-log-copy-turn’ copies the current turn to the kill
ring.  A "turn" is delimited by ‘##’ headings: from the current ‘##
User’ or ‘## Assistant’ heading up to (but not including) the next one.
This is useful for extracting a specific message from a conversation to
paste elsewhere.  The keybinding is ‘w’.


File: agent-log.info,  Node: Summary generation,  Next: Session rename,  Prev: Copying,  Up: Commands

4.7 Summary generation
======================

The command ‘agent-log-summarize-sessions’ generates AI summaries for
all ordinary sessions that do not yet have a current one.  It requires
the ‘gptel’ package.  The command processes sessions sequentially,
sending each conversation's text to the configured LLM (*note Summary
generation options: Summary generation (1).) and storing the response in
the index.  Summary requests disable ‘gptel-use-tools’ so that globally
configured tools (e.g.  MCP servers) are not included in the request.

   Each summary consists of two parts: a one-line summary (up to 80
characters) used in the session browser display, and a paragraph summary
(3-5 sentences) shown at the top of rendered buffers.  The LLM is
instructed to return a JSON object with ‘oneline’ and ‘summary’ fields.

   Progress is displayed in the echo area, showing which pending summary
update is running, how many sessions were discovered in the archive,
which session is being summarized, and the model in use.  The
pending-update count is the number of sessions lacking a current
summary, not the total number of sessions in the archive.  If you invoke
‘agent-log-summarize-sessions’ while summarization is already running,
it stops the current run instead.  You can also stop explicitly with
‘agent-log-stop-summarize-sessions’.

   Summaries are persisted in the index file together with a fingerprint
and source file state for the conversation text they describe, so they
survive across Emacs sessions and are refreshed only when the underlying
conversation changes.  Rendered Markdown files also include the
paragraph summary below the session header when a usable summary is
available; if a background summary finishes after rendering, the
rendered file is updated in place.  Because the summary is stored in the
file itself, opening a log displays it exactly once rather than
re-inserting it.  If the JSONL file state changes but the fingerprint
still matches, the index metadata is refreshed in place without another
LLM request on the per-session summarization path.  This matters for
metadata-only rewrites, such as moving a Codex session log to a new
project: the JSONL size and modification time can change even though the
user/assistant conversation text has not.  Archive-wide commands do not
preflight every stale fingerprint in the live Emacs process, because
doing so can require rereading large transcripts before any LLM request
starts.  Instead, archive-wide summarization and search treat stored
summaries with a current-version conversation fingerprint as usable even
when the cached JSONL size or modification time is stale; exact refresh
remains on the targeted per-session path.  If you resume a previously
summarized session and continue working, the next targeted summarization
path treats the old summary as stale and generates a replacement.  The
fingerprint covers all summary-relevant user and assistant text, not
only the capped text sent to the LLM, so resumed work appended to a long
session is still detected.  Legacy summaries with an older fingerprint
are validated against the current conversation text and upgraded in
place when that individual session is processed; summaries without a
fingerprint are refreshed once because the package cannot prove what
conversation text they describe.

* Menu:

* Automatic sync and summarization on session end::


