4.7.1 Automatic sync and summarization on session end
-----------------------------------------------------

These user options control what happens automatically when a Claude Code
or Codex session stops producing output.  Claude Code requires a ‘Stop’
hook configured in Claude Code's settings (see the ‘claude-code’
documentation for hook setup).  Codex requires ‘codex-mode’ with
‘codex-enable-hooks’ enabled so ‘codex-event-hook’ receives ‘Stop’
events.

   When ‘agent-log-auto-sync-sessions’ is non-nil, the stopped session
is rendered automatically.  If the event cannot be resolved to a
specific session, automatic sync is skipped.

   When ‘agent-log-auto-summarize-sessions’ is non-nil, the stopped
session is summarized automatically in a background ‘emacs --quick
--batch’ subprocess, so the running Emacs stays responsive while the LLM
request and index update complete.  The session-end path resolves the
stopped session by file lookup and parses only that session's JSONL file
for project, timestamp, and first-message metadata; it does not scan the
full archive.  The worker does not load the user's init file; it loads a
private temporary state file containing the relevant ‘agent-log’ and
‘gptel’ runtime configuration, including whether
‘agent-log-auto-rename-sessions’ is enabled, then deletes that file.
Successful background workers finish silently; failures are reported in
the echo area.  If auto-sync is also enabled, the background summarizer
is launched after sync completes.  If the event cannot be resolved to a
specific session, automatic summarization is skipped quietly.  Workers
start from ‘temporary-file-directory’ rather than the current buffer's
directory, because they address their state file absolutely and never
use the working directory; this keeps summarization working after a
project directory has been deleted or renamed, which would otherwise
stop the worker from starting at all.

   If older index entries point at files under the ‘unknown’ rendered
project, run ‘M-x agent-log-repair-rendered-index’.  The command
re-renders those sessions from their JSONL files, writes corrected
project-path Markdown files, and updates the rendered index.  It does
not delete the old ‘unknown’ files.

   Automatic summarization also runs a periodic backlog sweep so
sessions created outside the Emacs lifecycle hooks, such as external
Codex invocations, are eventually summarized.  The sweep is controlled
by ‘agent-log-auto-summarize-sweep-interval’ (default 900 seconds) and
‘agent-log-auto-summarize-sweep-limit’ (default 20 sessions per sweep).
The timer is self-rescheduling rather than a long-lived repeating timer,
so stale triggered timer objects are replaced automatically after each
firing.  Each sweep runs in a separate batch Emacs process; the running
Emacs only starts the worker and does not scan or summarize the archive
itself.  If AI search or session browsing sees missing usable summaries
while automatic summarization is enabled, it repairs a stale sweep timer
if needed and starts a non-overlapping sweep that prioritizes the
session set that exposed the missing summaries before continuing.  Set
‘agent-log-auto-summarize-sweep-interval’ to nil to disable backlog
sweeps while keeping session-end summaries enabled.

   The hook handlers (‘agent-log-claude--session-end-handler’ and
‘agent-log-codex--session-end-handler’) are defined in the backend files
and installed via ‘eval-after-load’ when either defcustom is set before
the backend is loaded.  The Codex backend also installs a session
identity recorder on ‘codex-event-hook’ and a start hook that clears
stale buffer-local identity.  It rechecks these hooks before ‘codex’
dispatches lifecycle events, so another package resetting
‘codex-event-hook’ does not silently disable automatic summaries.

   Archive-wide manual summarization excludes sessions with a live agent
process by session ID.  Each backend's ‘agent-log--active-session-ids’
method provides the IDs of currently running sessions.  For Codex
buffers, the backend uses the same hook-recorded ID, resume-ID,
project-plus-launch-time, and visible-transcript matching logic as
‘agent-log-open-current-session’ (*note Session browsing::).  Claude
buffers whose ‘claude-code-extras’ status data has not been initialized
yet are ignored until a session ID is available.  Targeted automatic
summarization may summarize the stopped session even while its terminal
buffer remains live, because the lifecycle event identifies that
specific session.  If summarization is already running when another
session stops, the in-progress batch continues undisturbed.


