4.10 Session resumption
=======================

The command ‘agent-log-resume-session’ resumes the session displayed in
the current buffer in the coding agent.  The command extracts the
session ID from the buffer (either from the buffer-local variable or
from the front-matter comment) and dispatches to the buffer's backend
via the ‘agent-log--resume-session’ generic function.  If a rendered
Markdown file was opened directly rather than through ‘agent-log’, the
command also reads the front-matter source path to recover the backend
and source JSONL file before resuming.  For Claude Code sessions, this
requires the ‘claude-code’ package and passes the session ID with the
‘--resume’ flag, starting in the session's project directory when
possible.  When the project path stored in Claude's transcript or
‘history.jsonl’ no longer exists, the backend falls back to the encoded
project directory under ‘~/.claude/projects/’ and uses the matching
existing project directory.  The project directory is normalized with a
trailing slash to match the format returned by ‘claude-code--directory’,
ensuring that buffer names, concurrent session detection, and modeline
display all work correctly.  For Codex sessions, this requires the
‘codex’ package.  Agent Log first verifies the ID against the current
canonical catalog, then passes both that ID and the catalog's exact
transcript path to app-server ‘thread/resume’.  It does not rescan
rollouts or fall back to another same-ID file if the canonical path
disappears.  The CLI backend uses ‘codex resume’ for the same
catalog-validated ID.  The keybinding is ‘r’ in ‘agent-log-mode’.  When
the *note agent integration: Agent integration. is active, resuming
additionally behaves as described there: live sessions are switched to
rather than duplicated, and new resumes are routed through the ‘agent’
dispatcher.


