5 Agent integration
*******************

Agent Log owns the durable archive; the optional agent
(https://github.com/benthamite/agent) package owns live session control
(buffers, accounts, lifecycle state, teardown).  The bridge module
‘agent-log-agent.el’ connects the two when both are loaded, regardless
of load order: ‘agent-log.el’ requires it only after both the ‘agent’
and ‘agent-log’ features are available, so loading Agent Log alone never
loads or requires ‘agent’, and Agent Log remains fully functional
standalone.  The bridge uses only public ‘agent’ API and adds ‘:around’
methods to existing Agent Log generics, so removing it restores
standalone behavior exactly.

   With the bridge active:

   • Opening the current session's log resolves the transcript from the
     buffer's authoritative native session id, which ‘agent’ maintains
     from the Claude status line and Codex hook events.  The standalone
     heuristics (visible-text transcript matching, newest project JSONL,
     ‘history.jsonl’ project match, Codex process and launch-time
     matching) remain as fallbacks for buffers whose id is not yet
     known.
   • The session browser tags sessions that are currently live with
     their state — ‘[busy]’, ‘[waiting]’, ‘[background-waiting]’, or
     ‘[unknown]’ — in the ‘agent-log-live-state’ face.  There are two
     extension points: ‘agent-log-live-session-info-function’ answers
     about one session at a time, and
     ‘agent-log-live-session-info-table-function’ returns a snapshot of
     every live session at once.  The bridge installs both, and the
     browser prefers the snapshot whenever it is installed, even if the
     snapshot comes back empty.  The tag is a column like any other: it
     is padded to the width of the widest tag on display, so the date,
     project, and size columns line up whether or not a row is live, and
     it disappears entirely when no displayed session is live.
     Selecting a session still opens the rendered log; liveness only
     annotates.
   • The ‘agent’ session switcher shows each live session's stored
     one-line summary after the session name, dimmed and aligned into a
     column.  The bridge supplies that text through
     ‘agent-session-annotation-functions’, the abnormal hook ‘agent’
     runs when it builds the switcher; ‘agent’ itself knows nothing
     about summaries.  Loading the bridge adds its own function to that
     hook with ‘add-hook’, so an annotation function you installed
     yourself keeps working whichever loads first, and the switcher
     shows whichever of you answers first.  ‘remove-hook’ takes the
     bridge's function back out if you would rather annotate the
     switcher some other way.  The text comes from
     ‘agent-log-session-oneline’, which reads an in-memory cache rather
     than the rendered index, so opening the switcher never waits on a
     multi-megabyte file.  A session shows no annotation at all when the
     archive has not summarized it yet, when it is marked as having no
     conversation to summarize, or when ‘agent’ does not yet know its
     native session id; such sessions look exactly as they did before.
     Because the background summary sweep skips sessions it believes are
     live (see below), a live session's annotation describes its
     conversation as of the last time it was summarized, which can be
     hours behind.
   • Resuming a session that is already live switches to its existing
     buffer, with a message naming its state, instead of starting a
     duplicate process on the same conversation.
   • Resuming an inactive session is routed through
     ‘agent-start-session’, so the resumed process gets ‘agent’'s
     account handling, lifecycle registration, teardown, and state
     tracking.  Codex resumes keep the catalog validation and
     exact-transcript-path behavior described in *note Session
     resumption::.
   • Live-session id listing (used to protect running sessions from
     background summarization) comes from ‘agent’'s session registry
     instead of backend heuristics, which for Codex also avoids reading
     the thread catalog.

   The cache behind the switcher annotations is filled by
‘agent-log-refresh-session-onelines’, which rereads the rendered index
and keeps only the one-line summaries, so a consumer that wants a label
does not hold the whole index in memory.  The rebuild is skipped when
the index file's size and modification time are unchanged since the last
one.  The bridge runs the rebuild from a repeating idle timer whose
delay is the user option ‘agent-log-oneline-refresh-idle-delay’, in
seconds, defaulting to ‘5’.  A repeating idle timer runs at most once
per idle period, so the rebuild happens on each pause that lasts at
least that long, not once every five seconds of a long pause.  A firing
whose index is unchanged costs a single file-attribute check; a firing
after a change rereads and reparses the whole index, which takes roughly
90 ms on a 6 MB archive.  Lower the delay to have a newly written
summary reach the switcher after a shorter pause; raise it so that only
longer pauses pay for the reparse.  Setting the option to ‘nil’, or to a
number that is not positive, cancels the timer altogether, and the
switcher then shows whatever the cache last held.  The option
reschedules the timer when it is set, so a change made through Customize
or ‘setopt’ takes effect at once.

   A corrupt rendered index makes the rebuild signal rather than replace
good summaries with an empty cache, so the switcher keeps showing the
annotations it already had.  Emacs reports the error from the timer as
an ordinary message, once per pause rather than every few seconds; with
‘debug-on-error’ set, each of those firings raises the debugger and
shows a backtrace instead.  The timer survives the error, and the
index's file state is recorded only after a successful rebuild, so every
later firing rereads the whole index until the index is valid again — at
which point annotations resume by themselves, with nothing to restart.

   Browsing history stays lazy in both directions: the ‘agent’
transients never read the historical catalog, and building the switcher
reads only memory.  The idle refresh above is the one piece of bridge
work that runs on its own; everything else waits for an explicit action.
On the ‘agent’ side, the shared ‘agent-history’ command (bound to ‘l’ in
‘agent-menu’) opens ‘agent-log-menu’ on demand.


