6.1 Coverage
============

The built-in pattern list (‘agent-log-redact-patterns’) covers AWS
access keys, GitHub tokens (classic, ‘ghs_’, and fine-grained PATs),
GitLab personal access tokens, Slack tokens, generic ‘sk-’ API keys
(with a more specific ‘sk-ant-’ rule for Anthropic keys that fires
first), Google API keys (‘AIza...’), JSON Web Tokens, database
connection strings with embedded credentials (‘postgres’, ‘mysql’,
‘mongodb’, etc.), Google OAuth client secrets, PEM private-key blocks,
and generic ‘api_key’ / ‘password’ / ‘token’ assignments with
high-entropy values.  Add site-specific patterns via
‘agent-log-redact-extra-patterns’ (same ‘(REGEX . LABEL)’ alist format);
suppress known-safe matches via ‘agent-log-redact-allowlist’ (a list of
literal strings).


File: agent-log.info,  Node: Integration,  Next: Commands (1),  Prev: Coverage,  Up: Secret redaction

6.2 Integration
===============

Redaction is applied at two layers:

  1. _Render-time_ via ‘:filter-return’ advice on
     ‘agent-log--render-entry’, so every rendered entry passes through
     the redactor on its way to the Markdown file.  Covers both the
     full-render path (‘agent-log--render-to-file’) and the live-update
     path (‘agent-log--append-rendered-line’).
  2. _Source-level_ via ‘agent-log-redact-scrub-jsonl’, which walks each
     .jsonl under ‘agent-log-redact-jsonl-roots’ (Claude Code's
     ‘~/.claude/projects/’, Codex's ‘~/.codex/sessions/’), parses each
     line as JSON, and rewrites non-opaque string values through
     ‘agent-log-redact-text’ atomically.  JSON object keys and values
     under ‘agent-log-redact-opaque-json-keys’ are preserved unchanged.
     Files modified within ‘agent-log-redact-active-session-seconds’ are
     skipped to avoid racing with an active session.

   Source-level scrubbing is the authoritative redaction for plaintext
transcript fields: once the JSONL is clean, every downstream rendering
of that session is automatically clean, and there is no plain-text copy
of the secret on local disk.  Session-resume remains safe only if
authenticated or encrypted resume fields are preserved byte-for-byte; by
default, keys such as ‘encrypted_content’, ‘signature’, ‘ciphertext’,
‘nonce’, ‘mac’, ‘tag’, and suffixed variants such as
‘thinking_signature’ are treated as opaque.

   Neither layer is a substitute for preventing secrets from entering
the transcript in the first place (that is the job of an upstream
PreToolUse hook).  They are defense in depth: prevention at the edge,
redaction at the core, and redaction at the view.


File: agent-log.info,  Node: Commands (1),  Next: User options (1),  Prev: Integration,  Up: Secret redaction

6.3 Commands
============

‘agent-log-redact-rebuild-all’ deletes every rendered ‘.md’ file under
‘agent-log-rendered-directory’, clears the index, and re-runs
‘agent-log-sync-sessions’ so every non-ignored canonical session is
re-rendered through the redactor.  Use after enabling redaction or after
changing the pattern list.

   ‘agent-log-redact-existing-in-place’ is a faster alternative for the
first-time migration.  It iterates every already-rendered ‘.md’ file,
runs its contents through ‘agent-log-redact-text’, and rewrites only
files whose content actually changed — no JSONL re-parsing.  Much faster
than ‘agent-log-redact-rebuild-all’ on large archives.  Sessions whose
JSONL has grown since their last render are not refreshed by this
command; run ‘agent-log-sync-sessions’ afterward to pick up any new
content through the advice-based redactor.

   ‘agent-log-redact-scrub-jsonl’ is the source-level scrubber.  It
walks every directory in ‘agent-log-redact-jsonl-roots’ recursively, and
for every ‘.jsonl’ older than ‘agent-log-redact-active-session-seconds’
examines each line.  Idempotency is enforced at three layers.  First,
‘agent-log-redact-text’ skips any regex match whose captured text
already contains a ‘[REDACTED:...]’ substring, so a pattern with a
permissive character class cannot swallow and re-hash its own
placeholder (e.g.  the ‘db-password’ pattern captures ‘[^@/ \n]+’ as the
password, which would otherwise match a previously-redacted placeholder
on a subsequent pass).  Second, lines that contain no pattern match at
all are left verbatim — no JSON round-trip.  Third, lines that match
syntactically but whose recursive walk redacts no actual string are also
left verbatim (this covers matches inside already-redacted placeholders
and opaque fields such as Codex ‘encrypted_content’).  Only lines where
at least one non-opaque string value is actually rewritten trigger JSON
re-serialization.  The file is rewritten atomically (temporary file +
rename) only when at least one line actually changed.  Both fast paths
are required because JSON parse + serialize is not guaranteed to be
byte-identical for all valid inputs — unconditional re-serialization
would cause spurious drift across re-runs.  Lines that fail to parse as
JSON are preserved unchanged, so malformed or truncated entries survive
scrubbing.  The write path disables Emacs lockfiles so concurrent
scrubber invocations do not deadlock on stale ‘.#’ lock symlinks.  Run
once after enabling redaction to clean historical sessions, and
periodically afterward (manually, or via a cron-like schedule) to scrub
newly-completed sessions.

   ‘agent-log-redact-preview’ lists every potential match in the current
buffer — with its label, position, and a truncated preview — in a
dedicated report buffer.  Useful for tuning patterns or adding entries
to ‘agent-log-redact-allowlist’ when a legitimate value triggers a false
positive.


File: agent-log.info,  Node: User options (1),  Prev: Commands (1),  Up: Secret redaction

6.4 User options
================

‘agent-log-redact-enabled’ (default ‘t’) toggles redaction globally.
Set to ‘nil’ to bypass the redactor without unloading the module.

   ‘agent-log-redact-patterns’ is the built-in ‘(REGEX . LABEL)’ alist.
Each entry is applied in order.  If a regex contains a capture group,
only the first capture group is replaced; otherwise the whole match is
replaced.  Use a capture group when you want to preserve surrounding
context (e.g.  the ‘password=’ prefix in a ‘password=VALUE’ match).

   ‘agent-log-redact-extra-patterns’ is appended to the built-in list on
every scan.  Use it to add site-specific secret formats without editing
the built-in.

   ‘agent-log-redact-allowlist’ is a list of literal strings that are
never redacted, even when matched by a pattern.  Use to suppress known
false positives such as public test keys.

   ‘agent-log-redact-hash-salt’ is an optional salt prepended to secrets
before hashing.  An empty salt (the default) is safe — the 8-char
truncation already makes recovery impractical.  Set a non-empty salt if
you sync the same logs to multiple machines and want placeholders to
differ across them.

   ‘agent-log-redact-jsonl-roots’ lists the directories the source-level
scrubber walks.  Defaults to Claude Code's ‘~/.claude/projects/’ and
Codex's ‘~/.codex/sessions/’.

   ‘agent-log-redact-opaque-json-keys’ lists JSON keys whose values are
never source-redacted.  This protects authenticated ciphertext and
signature fields used by agent resume protocols.  Keys ending in
‘_signature’, ‘_ciphertext’, ‘_nonce’, ‘_mac’, or ‘_tag’ are also
treated as opaque.

   ‘agent-log-redact-active-session-seconds’ controls how
recently-modified a .jsonl must be before the scrubber skips it.
Default 300 (five minutes).  Prevents racing with a session that is
still writing.

   ‘agent-log-auto-redact-sessions’, when non-nil, scrubs JSONL
automatically after each Claude Code session ends.  Installs a handler
on ‘claude-code-event-hook’ that fires on ‘:type stop’ and, after
‘agent-log-redact-active-session-seconds’ plus 30 seconds, launches an
‘emacs --batch’ subprocess that runs ‘agent-log-redact-scrub-jsonl’.
Using a subprocess is essential: scrubbing thousands of files inline in
the interactive Emacs would block the UI for minutes.  Codex has no
equivalent event hook, so Codex sessions are only scrubbed on manual
invocation.  This is the companion to ‘agent-log-auto-sync-sessions’ and
‘agent-log-auto-summarize-sessions’ — same pattern, different action.


File: agent-log.info,  Node: agent-log-mode,  Next: How it works,  Prev: Secret redaction,  Up: Top

7 ‘agent-log-mode’
******************

‘agent-log-mode’ is the major mode used for rendered session buffers.
It derives from ‘markdown-view-mode’, inheriting Markdown fontification
in a read-only buffer.  Buffers are never marked as modified, so ‘q’
(‘quit-window’) closes them without prompting to save.  The mode
activates ‘outline-minor-mode’ with a regexp that matches Markdown
headings (=## =, =### =, =#### =), enabling section folding.

   Two invisibility specs are added: ‘agent-log-collapsed’ (which shows
the heading but hides the body) and ‘agent-log-hidden’ (which hides both
heading and body).  These are used by the section visibility commands
(*note Section visibility::) and the display configuration options
(*note Display of thinking and tool sections::).

   When a buffer in this mode is killed, the file watcher is cleaned up
and the index is updated with the final JSONL file size.

   The full keymap is:

Key     Command                      Description
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘n’     ‘agent-log-next-turn’        Jump to the next User/Assistant heading
‘p’     ‘agent-log-previous-turn’    Jump to the previous heading
‘TAB’   ‘agent-log-toggle-section’   Toggle fold of section at point
‘C’     ‘agent-log-collapse-all’     Collapse all tool/thinking sections
‘E’     ‘agent-log-expand-all’       Expand all sections
‘g’     ‘agent-log-refresh’          Re-render the buffer from scratch
‘w’     ‘agent-log-copy-turn’        Copy the current turn to the kill ring
‘r’     ‘agent-log-resume-session’   Resume the session in the coding agent
‘?’     ‘agent-log-menu’             Open the transient menu
‘q’     ‘quit-window’                Close the buffer

